      "width": 420.0,
      "height": 790.0,
      "z_value": -9.92800000000004,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "label": "So `Theme.iconLoopOff` etc. aren't defined anywhere either \u2014 they just hit the `_ThemeMeta.__getattr__` and return `_MISSING_ICON`, then `Theme.icon()` gets the sentinel and returns a circle with the fallback_color. That's the expected pattern \u2014 the fallback color is what makes the button visible. The actual `.ico` files are optional visual upgrades that users configure in settings.toml.\n\nSo my implementation is correct \u2014 `Theme.iconBorderOff` and `Theme.iconBorderOn` will use the fallback colors `#7a8a9a` and `#e1d5c6` respectively, producing colored circles. If the user later registers the icons in settings.toml, the proper icons will appear.\n\nTask 1 is done. Now Task 2:",
      "depth_front": false,
      "node_tint": "#2a2f3a"
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "claude_response",
      "title": "Response",
      "uuid": "975fe2d768d348bc961be08539f68edc",
      "x": 5600.7933285980125,
      "y": -1031.2510306389272,
      "width": 420.0,
      "height": 510.0,
      "z_value": -9.92900000000004,
      "ports_visible": false,
      "shelf_visible": true,
      "label": "I see \u2014 the `Theme.icon()` method takes a *filename* string, but `Theme.iconBorderOn` will return `_MISSING_ICON` since the icon isn't registered in settings. The filename needs to come from settings.toml's `[theme.icons]` section. Since settings.toml doesn't exist in the repo (it's generated), and the icons section has no defaults, I need to pass the filename directly rather than going through the Theme attribute. Let me check how the loop icons work:",
      "depth_front": false,
      "node_tint": "#2a2f3a"
    },
    {
      "node_id": 0,
      "node_type": "claude_response",
      "title": "Response",
